**[Critical]** When `allowPrivateNetworkHosts` is true, the entire IP-address branch is skipped here — `isBlockedAddress()` never runs for IP literals. The only remaining protection is the `BLOCKED_HOSTS` string-match check above, which matches `'169.254.169.254'` exactly but does **not** catch IPv6-mapped forms like `::ffff:a9fe:a9fe` (the WHATWG serialization of `::ffff:169.254.169.254`). — Failure scenario: with `allowPrivateNetworkHooks: true`, a hook URL `http://[::ffff:169.254.169.254]/latest/meta-data` passes both the `BLOCKED_HOSTS` check (string mismatch) and the skipped IP-range check, reaching the cloud metadata endpoint. The documentation states metadata endpoints "remain blocked" but this is not enforced for IPv6-mapped variants.

Suggested fix: always run `isBlockedAddress()` for IP literals regardless of the flag — it correctly handles IPv4-mapped IPv6 via `extractMappedIPv4`. Only skip the broader private-range block for hostnames.

```suggestion
    if (this.isIpAddress(hostname)) {
      // Remove brackets from IPv6 addresses for isBlockedAddress
      const cleanHostname = hostname.replace(/^\[|\]$/g, '');
      if (isBlockedAddress(cleanHostname)) {
        debugLogger.debug(`URL blocked: IP ${hostname} is blocked`);
        return true;
      }
      // When allowPrivateNetworkHosts is true, non-blocked IP literals
      // (e.g. 172.16.x.x) are allowed — skip further checks.
      if (this.allowPrivateNetworkHosts) {
        return false;
      }
