discussion_comments.comment_body:
**[Critical]** `isAsyncOperator`'s backward scan is not escape-aware: a backslash-escaped `\>` or `\<` before `&` is misread as a redirection operator, so the async `&` is not split and whatever follows it escapes the per-segment permission check. Failure scenario: `echo a \> & rm -rf /` stays joined — bash runs `echo a >` in the background and `rm -rf /` as a separate foreground command. If an allow rule matches `echo`, the `rm` half is never independently checked — a permission bypass.

```suggestion
  for (let j = index - 1; j >= 0; j--) {
    const ch = command[j]!;
    if (/\s/.test(ch)) {
      continue;
    }
    if (ch === '>' || ch === '<') {
      let backslashes = 0;
      for (let k = j - 1; k >= 0 && command[k] === '\\'; k--) {
        backslashes++;
      }
      return backslashes % 2 === 1;
    }
    return true;
  }
```

<details>
<summary>中文说明</summary>

`isAsyncOperator` 的反向扫描未考虑转义：`&` 前的反斜杠转义 `\>` 或 `\<` 会被误判为重定向操作符，导致异步 `&` 不被切分，其后的命令逃逸逐段权限检查。失败场景：`echo a \> & rm -rf /` 保持为单一分段——bash 将 `echo a >` 放到后台，`rm -rf /` 作为独立前台命令执行。若存在匹配 `echo` 的放行规则，`rm` 部分永远不会被独立检查——构成权限绕过。

</details>

