discussion_comments.comment_body:
Confirmed and fixed in 26f7c99. This was the finding worth the most — thank you.

I measured all three states rather than reasoning about them, calling `extractShellOperationsAcrossCommand(cmd, "/repo")`:

| | `cd /tmp & echo {} > settings.json` |
| --- | --- |
| `main` (3209b89) | `[]` |
| this PR before the fix | `write_file:/tmp/settings.json` |
| **after 26f7c99** | **`write_file:/repo/settings.json`** |

So the shape is slightly different from a plain regression, and I want to be accurate about it. On `main` the whole string was one segment, `tokenize` saw `words[0] === "cd"`, and `resolveCdTargetCwd` returned `static` — which makes `walkCompoundCommand` `continue` and drop the segment entirely. The write was not misattributed, it was **not seen at all**. This PR made it visible but pointed it at the `cd` target, which is what you caught. Either way the real write to `<cwd>/settings.json` went unchecked, and now it does not.

### The fix

`splitCompoundCommand` returns bare strings, so the walker had no way to tell `cd /tmp & …` from `cd /tmp && …`. Added `splitCompoundCommandSegments`, which returns the same split plus the operator that ended each segment:

```ts
export interface CompoundCommandSegment {
  command: string;
  /** The operator that terminated this segment, or `""` at end of input. */
  terminator: string;
}
```

`splitCompoundCommand` is now a projection of it (`.map(s => s.command)`), so its behaviour and every existing caller are untouched. `walkCompoundCommand` skips the cwd shift when `terminator === "&"`:

```ts
const backgrounded = terminator === "&";
const cdTarget = resolveCdTargetCwd(sub, effectiveCwd, cwdUnknown);
if (cdTarget.kind === "static") {
  if (!backgrounded) {
    effectiveCwd = cdTarget.cwd;
    cwdUnknown = cdTarget.cwdUnknown;
  }
  continue;
}
```

The `dynamic` branch gets the same treatment, and there it is a strict improvement in precision: `cd "$TARGET" & echo {} > settings.json` no longer marks the write `cwdUnknown`, because a backgrounded `cd` cannot have moved the cwd regardless of what `$TARGET` held.

### Tests

Five, in `shell-semantics.test.ts` — two backgrounded forms (absolute and relative target), the backgrounded dynamic target, and two over-correction guards that pass before and after: foreground `cd /tmp && …` still resolves to `/tmp/settings.json`, and foreground `cd "$TARGET" && …` still sets `cwdUnknown`. Plus `cd /tmp & cd /var && echo {} > settings.json` → `/var/settings.json`, so a foreground `cd` after a backgrounded one still applies.

### Scope note

The same subshell reasoning applies to `|`: `cd /tmp | echo x` also runs in a subshell and does not move the parent cwd, and `walkCompoundCommand` treats it as foreground today. That one predates this PR — `|` has always been a boundary — so I left it out rather than widen this change. It is now a one-token fix on the `backgrounded` line if you would like it as a follow-up.

Full permissions suite: 751 passed (9 files). `prettier --check`, `eslint --max-warnings 0`, and `tsc --noEmit` clean.

